5.2  The OOM killer

TL;DR
  - When RAM and swap are both used up, the kernel's OOM ("out of memory")
    killer picks a process and kills it to get memory back.
  - It mostly picks the biggest user of memory, adjusted by each process's
    oom_score_adj (-1000 to 1000).
  - The victim gets SIGKILL: no warning, no cleanup. The kernel log says who.

      journalctl -k | grep -i "out of memory"
      cat /proc/1234/oom_score

On your machine
  RAM       10.5 GiB available of 15.5 GiB (68%) · 1.1 GiB completely free
  Cache     7.5 GiB in buff/cache, 604.0 MiB of it shared (tmpfs)
  Swap      256.0 MiB used of 8.0 GiB · swappiness 60

Pitfalls
  - On Fedora and Ubuntu, systemd-oomd can kill a whole app earlier, when memory
    pressure stays high: check journalctl -u systemd-oomd.
  - In a container or a service with a memory limit, the OOM killer acts inside
    that limit, even if the machine has free RAM.
  - A machine that swaps hard can stay frozen for minutes before the OOM killer
    acts.
  - oom_score_adj = -1000 makes a process immune. Use it sparingly, or something
    else important gets killed instead.
  - Reading the kernel log may need sudo or membership of the adm or
    systemd-journal group.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 5.2 --why

Related
  Commands  journalctl, dmesg
  Paths     /proc/PID/oom_score, /proc/PID/oom_score_adj
  Hub       idk memory Memory
  See also  idk 5.3 Swap and swappiness
  See also  idk 4.2 Signals
  See also  idk 10.3 dmesg and the kernel log
